# D22 — the input-path repairs: gsl_tp I2C write contract + firmware loader +
# init failure propagation + Q5 volume-key reversal.
# Task (binding): .specs/hera-d22-input-diagnosis.md. Report:
# .specs/forge-d22-input-report.md (host-test + build evidence).
#
# THE DEFECTS (each reproduced independently on the host before this patch —
# hera-d22-input-diagnosis §"Source root defects"; the isolated probes
# q5-gsl-write-asan / q5-gsl-fw-asan abort under ASAN against the UNPATCHED
# function text, and build/d22_host_tests keeps reproducing them):
#  1. gsl_write_interface consumed a [reg|payload] buffer (buf[0] = reg, wire
#     len = num + 1) while EVERY caller (gsl_i2c_write_bytes, the firmware
#     loader, all chip helpers) passes pure payloads: the first payload byte
#     was destroyed and one byte was read past the caller's buffer on EVERY
#     I2C write (host ASAN: stack-buffer-overflow READ). On the wire this
#     means every register/command write carried wrong data + a trailing
#     garbage byte.
#  2. gsl_load_fw flushed on CONSTANT comparisons (`send_flag == 1 % burst`
#     / `== 0 % burst`) that only became true after a u8 wrap (~256 records
#     into a 129-byte staging buffer): cur ran past buf end (host ASAN:
#     stack-buffer-overflow WRITE at the next-page memcpy) and the page
#     selector was written from stale buffer bytes (cur never reset at page
#     records). The Q5 arrays are page-aligned (0xf0 selector + 32 data
#     records), so the real firmware download corrupted stack AND sent
#     garbage page selectors.
#  3. check_mem_data ignored the 0xb0 read result and the gsl_sw_init return
#     and `return result` was hardcoded 0; gsl_sw_init/the chip helpers/
#     gsl_load_fw/gsl_start_core discarded every transfer failure. The probe
#     therefore registered tpd_load_status = 1 on a controller it never
#     verified — a false-positive hardware success.
#  4. Q5 volume keys: live PTY capture shows physical VolumeUp emitting
#     KEY_VOLUMEDOWN under the D20 KPD_INIT_KEYMAP ({[0]=VOLDOWN,[1]=VOLUP}) —
#     the two active matrix slots are wired one row-shifted on this board.
#     The D22 contract reverses EXACTLY those two keys ([0]=VOLUP,
#     [1]=VOLDOWN) in the Q5-pinned DCT header; KPD_DL_KEY*/MT65XX_RECOVERY_
#     KEY (HW-keycode download/recovery contracts), the PMIC power key, the
#     debounce and every other row stay untouched. build/63_kernel_delta_input
#     .sh re-pins source + compiled (hal_kpd.o) state; the complementary
#     VolumeDown direction remains a hardware gate after flash.
#
# THE REPAIRS (vendor behaviour byte-preserved unless the defect IS it):
#  - gsl_write_interface: const payload + bounded staging buffer (≤128 B
#    payload + reg), register byte first, exactly num+1 wire bytes, caller
#    memory never mutated; MTK msg fields verbatim (addr mask + ENEXT, TEN
#    flags, timing 400); short transfers -> -EFAULT, bad sizes -> -EINVAL.
#  - gsl_i2c_write_bytes: payload-only contract (const), size-validated
#    [1..128] — the dead chunk loop (and its u8-offset wrap class) is gone.
#  - gsl_load_fw: bounded contiguous-register page-aware batches ≤
#    DMA_TRANS_LEN records (128 payload bytes); flush on full batch / page
#    selector / register discontinuity / end; page writes exactly reg 0xf0 +
#    4 payload bytes; words staged explicit little-endian; EVERY record
#    emitted exactly once in order incl. the final partial batch; signed
#    return, all failed/short transfers propagate.
#  - init path: check_mem_data propagates read + sw_init results; gsl_sw_init
#    propagates test/clear/reset/io/load/start failures (canary transport
#    failure falls straight to the required default load, which reports the
#    same dying link loudly; an unanswered canary keeps the vendor's bounded
#    3-try loop); after load/start a BOUNDED alive gate (reg 0xb0 byte0 ==
#    0x5a, ≤3 reads @200 ms) decides success — probe binds only on a
#    confirmed controller. Vendor delays, fingerprint selection, fw/tuning
#    arrays, and the blob call sequence (incl. io_control×2) unchanged.
#  - cust_kpd.h: the two active keys reversed per the live capture.
#
# TEST ARTIFACTS: build/d22_host_tests/run.sh extracts the OLD function text
# from the pre-D22 tree snapshot and the NEW text from the tree after this
# patch applies — REAL functions, not formulas — and proves: old helpers fail
# under ASAN (both above), repaired ones pass ASAN with payload immutability
# + exact byte streams, every extracted Q5 firmware array (CHECK_FW + 5
# configs, ALL record counts) emits exactly its records as page/data
# transfers (final partial batch, page transitions, discontinuities, full-
# batch cap, injected short/negative transports), and init failure NEVER
# reports success (bounded alive gate; transport/load/fingerprint failures
# refuse the probe).
# BUILD ARTIFACT (build/63_kernel_delta_input.sh): new O dir
# build-beta-touch-lcm-board3 on top of D1…D21, D22 invariants re-pinned at
# source level (incl. the reversed keymap so DrvGen regeneration cannot
# revert it), hal_kpd.o compiled keymap gate {VOLUP,VOLDOWN} = (115, 114),
# gsl .o carries the D22 rodata markers, D21 MBR gate re-asserted, board2
# O dir + image untouched (rollback immutable pair).
#
---


--- a/arch/arm/mach-mt6582/q5/dct/dct/inc/cust_kpd.h
+++ b/arch/arm/mach-mt6582/q5/dct/dct/inc/cust_kpd.h
@@ -75,12 +75,22 @@
 
 /* HW keycode [0 ~ 71] -> Linux keycode. D20: Q5 wiring per the pinned Lithium
  * blob's compiled kpd_keymap @0xc08d0e34 ({[0]=VOLDOWN,[1]=VOLUP}); the a106
- * lineage shifted the pair by one ([1]=VOLUP,[2]=VOLDOWN) — on the Q5 that would
- * read the matrix pads one-off (VOL+/- never emitted). */
+ * lineage shifted the pair by one ([1]=VOLUP,[2]=VOLDOWN).
+ * D22 (hera-d22-input-diagnosis §4): live PTY capture (VolumeUp DOWN/UP
+ * emitting KEY_VOLUMEDOWN on the D20 map) proves the two active matrix slots
+ * are wired one row-shifted vs the blob donor unit on this board — physical
+ * VOL+ sits at hw slot [0] and VOL- at hw slot [1]. The D22 contract reverses
+ * EXACTLY those two active keys ([0]=VOLUMEUP, [1]=VOLUMEDOWN); nothing else
+ * moves (the KPD_DL_KEY1/2 and MT65XX_RECOVERY_KEY macros are HW-keycode
+ * download/recovery contracts, PMIC pwr key 8 and the debounce stay, no
+ * HOME/system layout change). build/63_kernel_delta_input.sh re-pins the
+ * reversed map at source and compiled (hal_kpd.o) level so DrvGen
+ * regeneration cannot revert it; the complementary VolumeDown direction must
+ * still be hardware-verified after flash. */
 #define KPD_INIT_KEYMAP()	\
 {	\
-	[0] = KEY_VOLUMEDOWN,		\
-	[1] = KEY_VOLUMEUP,		\
+	[0] = KEY_VOLUMEUP,		\
+	[1] = KEY_VOLUMEDOWN,		\
 }	 
